Nausea and Vomiting
Palliative & End of Life
Unrelenting subjective nausea, Recurrent forceful emesis, Severe early satiety

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-040
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe dehydration and complete physical exhaustion secondary to intractable, continuous vomiting preventing all oral intake.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient has been experiencing unremitting, severe subjective nausea and recurrent forceful emesis for 48 hours, completely rejecting all fluids, food, and essential oral palliative medications. The patient is exhibiting severe clinical dehydration, orthostatic hypotension, and profound weakness. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to physically assess hydration status, administer stat parenteral antiemetics to bypass the non-functional gut, and prevent a severe metabolic crisis or acute renal failure prior to morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I cannot stop heaving. Even the smell of water makes me violently sick. I am completely drained and dizzy when I try to stand."
History of Present Illness: Over the past two days, the patient has suffered from a continuous, unrelenting, and severe subjective feeling of sickness completely explicitly independent of meals. This is accompanied by recurrent, forceful explicitly involuntary expulsion strictly of gastric contents, which has now transitioned into severely painful dry heaves explicitly producing no productive emesis. The adult child reports severe early satiety and intractable hiccups frequently accompanying the continuous subjective nausea. There is profound, rapid, and unintentional weight loss directly secondary explicitly to an absolute failure of oral intake. The patient complains of severe, clinically explicit dehydration distinctly manifesting as a dry mouth and extreme thirst they cannot quench. Generalized weakness is present, directly correlating with suspected severe underlying deranged electrolyte imbalances. There is a distinct exacerbation directly immediately explicitly provoked by smelling or seeing specifically strong foods.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to severe dizziness and weakness upon standing.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of syncopal falls; high risk of acute medication withdrawal due to inability to swallow baseline analgesia.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 mmHg (supine), 80/50 mmHg (standing) | HR: 115 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 97% on room air | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Patient is pale, lethargic, actively retching into a basin, with deeply sunken eyes and parched lips.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam confirms severe hypovolemia with a pronounced orthostatic blood pressure drop, resting tachycardia, and delayed capillary refill (>3 seconds). Skin turgor is explicitly poor, and mucous membranes are completely desiccated. Abdominal exam is soft, scaphoid, and non-tender without rebound or guarding, ruling out an acute surgical abdomen; bowel sounds are sluggish. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the unrelenting subjective nausea, the observed recurrent forceful emesis/retching, and the reported severe early satiety.
Environmental Observations: The room smells of emesis. The patient is lying flat, refusing to raise their head due to nausea.
Medication Reconciliation: The patient has vomited their regular oral opioid doses and oral metoclopramide, rendering them completely ineffective and establishing a high risk of an impending pain crisis.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Nausea and Vomiting - Status: Exacerbated (Severe Dehydration / Intractable Emesis)
Differentials:

Malignant bowel obstruction - Cannot be definitively ruled out without imaging, but the absence of colicky abdominal pain, absence of absolute constipation (patient passed flatus today), and lack of abdominal distension make a complete mechanical block less likely than centrally-driven or chemical nausea.

Raised intracranial pressure (brain metastases) - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary acute driver due to the absence of severe morning headache, focal neurological signs, or papilledema, though it remains a chronic risk.

Acute gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of diarrhea, fever, and the insidious, continuous nature of the nausea independent of infectious contacts.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, critical fluid and electrolyte crisis directly driven by intractable palliative nausea and vomiting. The objective findings of profound orthostatic hypotension, severe tachycardia, and completely desiccated mucous membranes confirm dangerous hypovolemia. Because the gut is entirely non-functional and rejecting all oral input, delaying medical assessment until standard hours would result in irreversible acute kidney injury, agonizing retching, and catastrophic syncopal falls. Immediate after-hours intervention was absolutely necessary to bypass the gastrointestinal tract, administer potent subcutaneous, centrally-acting antiemetics, and provide parenteral analgesia to cover the missed oral doses.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered a stat subcutaneous injection of haloperidol (targeting the chemoreceptor trigger zone for chemical nausea) and cyclizine (targeting vestibular/central pathways). Administered stat subcutaneous morphine to cover the missed oral analgesic doses and prevent a concurrent pain crisis. Prescribed a continuous subcutaneous infusion to maintain antiemetic control if the vomiting does not settle in 12 hours.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel the intense wave of nausea building up (trigger), completely close your eyes and explicitly instruct your child to place a cold, damp washcloth firmly on the back of your neck (behavior). The sudden cold stimulus rapidly distracts the brain's neural pathways, short-circuiting the nausea reflex and directly preventing the physical act of vomiting (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to community palliative care nursing for daily subcutaneous medication management and potential subcutaneous fluid administration (hypodermoclysis) tomorrow.
DME/Safety (Optional): Ensure an adequate supply of emesis bags. Bedside commode is strictly required due to the syncopal fall risk.
Follow-up: GP review via telehealth in 12 hours to assess the cessation of vomiting and tolerance of clear fluids.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient begins to vomit fresh, bright red blood or dark "coffee ground" material, develops severe, new abdominal pain that is hard to the touch, or becomes completely confused and unarousable, the adult child must call 000 immediately for emergency transfer.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe nausea is a complex chemical reaction driven by the illness; it requires strong, regular injected medications to control, rather than just eating ginger or drinking water.
Clinical Course: Until the vomiting is completely switched off by the injections, attempting to drink will only make the dehydration worse by triggering more fluid loss.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Adult Child: Do not attempt to force the patient to drink anything, not even sips of water, for the next two hours to allow the injection to work. Keep the patient strictly lying flat to prevent fainting.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your stomach is completely rejecting everything because the nausea center in your brain is stuck in the "on" position. Drinking water just irritates it further. The injections we gave bypass your stomach entirely, going straight to your brain to turn off that sickness switch. Once the switch is off, you will be able to rest and eventually sip fluids safely.
Actionable Steps: Keep your head completely still and flat. Use the cold washcloth technique. When the nausea finally breaks, start with just one teaspoon of ice chips every 15 minutes.

